"""Introduction, background, theory and methods text. Numbers that depend on runs come from N."""


def front(N):
    B = []
    h1 = lambda t: B.append({"type": "h1", "text": t})
    h2 = lambda t: B.append({"type": "h2", "text": t})
    p = lambda t: B.append({"type": "p", "text": t})
    eq = lambda t: B.append({"type": "eq", "text": t})

    h1("Introduction")
    p("Industrial operators increasingly want to learn from data held by other sites, machines and suppliers without moving that data. Federated learning trains a shared model by exchanging model updates rather than records {mcmahan2017,kairouz2021open}, and differentially private stochastic gradient descent (DP-SGD) bounds what those updates reveal about any single training record {abadi2016,dwork2014}. These tools have reached manufacturing and industrial security research, where federated detectors for machine faults, industrial Internet of Things (IIoT) intrusions and anomalous control behaviour are now common {ferrag2022,ma2022,kamali2025,padmavathi2025}. The reported results concentrate on accuracy and on the privacy mechanism. They say much less about the decision that follows a prediction, which in a factory is usually a choice between letting a part or a process continue, stopping it, or asking an engineer to look.")
    p("That decision is where trust is tested. A fault detector that misses rare failures can show high accuracy, and a privacy mechanism that adds noise to training can make those misses more frequent for exactly the minority classes that matter {bagdasaryan2019}. Engineers therefore need a statement about the error rate of automated decisions that holds for the deployed model, at a stated confidence and under a stated privacy guarantee. Distribution-free calibration methods such as split conformal prediction and Learn-then-Test give such statements for a pooled calibration set {vovk2022,angelopoulos2023,bates2021,angelopoulos2021ltt}. In a federation the calibration records are spread across sites and are as sensitive as the training records, so the calibration step itself must respect the privacy and secure aggregation design of the federation.")
    p("Recent work has started to join these threads. Federated conformal prediction pools calibration information across clients {lu2023,humbert2023,plassier2023}. Differentially private conformal prediction protects the calibration quantile in a central setting {angelopoulos2022private,romanus2026,wu2026} or under local differential privacy {penso2025}. Private federated calibration of probabilities has been studied with secure aggregation and server-side noise {maddock2025}, and federated calibration of intrusion detectors has been proposed without formal privacy {talpini2025}. To our knowledge no published method certifies asymmetric industrial decision risks (missed faults and false alarms) from calibration data that never leave the sites in clear form, with noise added by the clients themselves so that a secure-aggregation server never holds an unprotected statistic, and with a finite-sample guarantee that accounts for that noise.")
    p("The emerging literature on Industry 6.0 gives this question a wider setting. Authors use the term for industrial systems in which autonomous, distributed and increasingly AI-driven control operates across organisational boundaries {duggal2022,fernandezmiguel2025,lykov2025,babkin2025}. The term has no agreed definition and no policy status comparable to Industry 5.0, which the European Commission framed around human-centred, resilient and sustainable industry {breque2021,xu2021,ivanov2023,leng2022}. We treat Industry 6.0 as a set of proposed capabilities. The part we test is narrow and present-day: whether privacy-preserving collaborative learning can hand decisions to automation only where an error bound can be certified and route the remainder to people.")
    p(f"This paper asks how industrial systems can learn collaboratively while preserving data privacy, keeping automated decisions within stated error bounds and remaining usable under heterogeneity and attack. We make three contributions. First, we specify private aggregate risk control (PARC), a calibration protocol in which each site adds Skellam noise to a fixed histogram of calibration scores, the sums are formed by pairwise-mask secure aggregation, and the server certifies automatic-pass and automatic-alarm thresholds with a fixed-sequence test whose p-values are exact binomial-Skellam convolutions. We prove that the certified miss and false-alarm rates hold with probability at least 0.90, that the whole pipeline costs no more record-level privacy than its larger component when roles are assigned record by record, and that certification requires a minimum count of calibration events which we compute exactly. Second, we evaluate the protocol with DP federated training on eight industrial and security tasks covering {N['n_units']:,} analysed observations, including three physical CNC machines and five chronological sessions of an industrial control testbed, over {N['main_runs']} confirmatory runs, a Monte Carlo check with known score distributions and attack and robustness experiments. Third, we turn the evidence into operating rules for industrial governance: how much calibration evidence a site needs, when private calibration is cheap and when it is not, and what fails under drift.")
    p("The remainder of the paper reviews related work, states the theoretical framework and research questions, describes data and methods, reports results by research question and discusses implications and limits.")

    h1("Background and Related Work")
    h2("From Industry 4.0 to a proposed Industry 6.0")
    p("Industry 4.0 describes connected cyber-physical production in which machine data feed planning and control {xu2021}. Industry 5.0 adds human-centred work, resilience and sustainability as explicit goals and was set out by the European Commission in 2021 {breque2021}; later reviews treat it as a complement to the technology agenda of Industry 4.0 rather than a separate technology stage {xu2021,leng2022,ivanov2023}. Writing on Industry 6.0 is more recent and less settled. Duggal and colleagues sketched a roadmap built on hyper-connected, customer-driven manufacturing {duggal2022}; a Finnish white paper described antifragile, virtualised production with dynamic supply chains {annanpera2021}; Fernández-Miguel and colleagues proposed a cognitive manufacturing framework with AI-driven decentralised control and explicitly declined to fix a single definition {fernandezmiguel2025}; Lykov and colleagues demonstrated a generative-AI and multi-robot production chain under the same label {lykov2025}; and Babkin and colleagues framed it as human and machine counterparts in cyber-social systems {babkin2025}. Two features recur across these sources: decisions are delegated to distributed learning systems that span organisations, and human oversight changes from operating machines to supervising automated judgement. Both features raise the questions this paper measures, namely what a shared model reveals about each participant and when an automated decision can be trusted without a person.")
    h2("Privacy-preserving federated learning")
    p("Federated averaging (FedAvg) alternates local optimisation and server averaging {mcmahan2017}. Heterogeneous clients motivate proximal (FedProx) and control-variate (SCAFFOLD) corrections {li2020,karimireddy2020}, and malicious clients motivate coordinate-wise median and trimmed-mean aggregation {yin2018,blanchard2017}, which in turn can be attacked {fang2020,bagdasaryan2020}. Model updates leak training data through membership inference and gradient inversion {shokri2016,yeom2017,carlini2021,zhu2019,geiping2020}. DP-SGD clips each per-example gradient and adds Gaussian noise, with the cumulative guarantee tracked through Rényi differential privacy (RDP) {abadi2016,mironov2017}. Secure aggregation hides individual updates from the server by pairwise masks that cancel in the sum {bonawitz2017}; because the server then sees only sums, noise must be added by clients and must remain a valid privacy mechanism after summation, which motivates discrete mechanisms such as the distributed discrete Gaussian and the Skellam mechanism {kairouz2021ddg,agarwal2021}. A consequence noted in the survey literature is that secure aggregation and Byzantine-robust aggregation conflict, since robust rules need individual updates {kairouz2021open}. DP training also harms minority classes more than majority classes {bagdasaryan2019}, which matters for rare industrial faults.")
    h2("Calibration, selective prediction and risk control")
    p("Modern neural networks are often miscalibrated {guo2017}. Selective classification abstains when confidence is low {geifman2017}, and conformal prediction turns a held-out calibration set into prediction sets with finite-sample coverage under exchangeability {vovk2022,angelopoulos2023}. Risk-controlling prediction sets and Learn-then-Test generalise coverage to any monotone or testable risk by casting threshold selection as multiple hypothesis testing {bates2021,angelopoulos2021ltt}. For a monotone risk a fixed-sequence test that stops at the first non-rejection controls the family-wise error without a union bound, which is the property we use.")
    h2("Privacy in calibration and the gap this study addresses")
    p("«T:novelty» compares the closest methods. Private prediction sets compute a differentially private conformal quantile centrally and correct for the noise {angelopoulos2022private}; later centralised methods use quantile binary search or full-data stability arguments {romanus2026,wu2026}. Local differential privacy versions perturb labels or scores before collection {penso2025}. Federated conformal predictors aggregate calibration information under partial exchangeability {lu2023}, one-shot federated conformal prediction offers a locally private quantile-of-quantiles estimator {humbert2023}, and DP-FedCP adds client-side Gaussian noise to quantile-regression gradients with approximate coverage bounds {plassier2023}. Private federated post-hoc calibration combines secure aggregation with server-added Gaussian noise for user-level privacy and targets calibration error {maddock2025}. A Byzantine-robust federated conformal method uses histograms of calibration scores without formal privacy {lari2026}. In industrial applications, federated detectors report accuracy and sometimes a nominal privacy budget without composition or calibration analysis {padmavathi2025,ma2022,kamali2025}, and a federated calibrator for intrusion detection improves confidence estimates without formal privacy or risk control {talpini2025}.")
    p("The comparison shows four gaps. Existing private calibration methods control marginal coverage rather than the asymmetric pair of risks that industrial decisions involve, a missed fault and an unnecessary stop. Those that use secure aggregation rely on a trusted party to add noise, whereas a federation whose server sees only masked sums needs noise that each client adds and that remains exact after summation. The interaction between the privacy of training records and calibration records is not analysed, so practitioners cannot say what the full pipeline costs. Finally, none reports the minimum calibration evidence that a guarantee requires, which for rare industrial faults is the binding constraint. PARC addresses these gaps by design, and the experiments test whether the design holds on industrial data.")
    B.append({"type": "table_ref", "id": "novelty"})

    h1("Theoretical Framework and Research Questions")
    p("We combine two lenses. Socio-technical systems thinking, which the NIST AI Risk Management Framework adopts when it describes AI risk as arising from the interaction of technical components with people and organisations {nist2023}, directs attention to the hand-off between automation and human judgement. Trustworthy AI frameworks and the European AI Act add concrete requirements for that hand-off: risks must be measured and managed, and high-risk systems must allow effective human oversight, including the ability to override or stop the system {nist2023,euaiact2024}. From these lenses we derive a mechanism with three links («F:framework»). Privacy-preserving collaboration determines what each site discloses. Distributed learning determines how well the shared model ranks risky cases. Certified calibration converts ranking quality and calibration evidence into an explicit split between automated and escalated decisions. Human oversight receives the escalated cases and governs recalibration. Trust in this framework is multidimensional: it includes ranking quality, calibration, the validity of stated error bounds, robustness to corruption and attack, and the share of decisions that people must still make.")
    B.append({"type": "figure_ref", "id": "framework"})
    p("The framework yields five research questions. RQ1 asks how formal privacy budgets change the utility of federated industrial models. RQ2 asks how client heterogeneity, participation and machine or session structure change reliability. RQ3 asks which mechanisms preserve calibration, minority-event detection and dependable decisions under privacy and attack. RQ4 asks whether PARC provides valid risk control at an acceptable cost relative to established alternatives. RQ5 asks under which tasks, machines and shifts the guarantees remain useful. Four hypotheses were frozen before the confirmatory runs (Methods). H1 states that PARC thresholds respect their targets with the stated confidence when the score distributions are known. H2 states that, on held-out industrial data, PARC exceeds the miss-rate target less often than a private plug-in rule applied to the same noisy histograms. H3 states that private calibration at ε = 4 adds less than five percentage points of escalation relative to non-private Learn-then-Test on the same DP model. H4 states that realised risk rises on non-exchangeable tests. H1 and H2 could fail through an error in the derivation or implementation; H3 could fail if noise forces conservative thresholds; H4 could fail if the tested shifts are benign.")

    h1("Materials and Methods")
    h2("Data and evaluation designs")
    p("We used eight tasks («T:datasets»). Six were already registered in the project repository: AI4I 2020 predictive maintenance (10,000 synthetic records, 339 failures) {matzka2020}, SECOM semiconductor yield (1,567 observed runs, 590 sensors, 104 failures) {mccann2008}, Steel Plates Faults (1,941 observed plates, seven defect classes) {buscema2010}, Combined Cycle Power Plant output (9,568 observations) {tufekci2014}, gas turbine NOx emissions (36,733 hourly records in five annual files) {kaya2019} and RT-IoT2022 network flows (123,117 flows) {sharmila2023}. We added two sources chosen for their structure. The Bosch CNC machining data contain vibration recordings from three production milling machines across six half-year timeframes {tnani2022}; we cloned the official repository at commit d60581d (1,702 recordings, 70 anomalous; the UCI listing reports 2,700 instances, a difference we could not resolve from public documentation), extracted 21 vibration features per recording with the project's existing script and verified 90 axis-level statistics against the source HDF5 files with no mismatch. The HAI 21.03 industrial control dataset records a hardware-in-the-loop testbed under scripted attacks {shin2021}; we used its five attack sessions at commit 2a814ce, formed 13,400 non-overlapping 30-second windows with mean, standard deviation and change of 79 sensors, and labelled a window as attacked when any second carried the source attack flag (330 attack windows in 50 attack events). Other candidates (TON_IoT, Edge-IIoTset, CIC IoT 2023, N-BaIoT, NASA C-MAPSS and SWaT) were registered but not acquired because their hosts were unreachable from the execution environment, registration was required or redistribution is forbidden; none would have added a structure not already covered. Leakage controls removed AI4I failure-mode flags and identifiers, both gas-turbine targets from the predictors, Bosch recording length and labels, and all HAI label, session and event fields. The steel file was confirmed identical to a public mirror whose preparation script asserts exactly one defect flag per row before collapsing them to a class label.")
    p("Each record was assigned to training, calibration or test by an independent coin flip with probabilities 0.6, 0.2 and 0.2, which the privacy argument below requires. Where no natural client identity exists (AI4I, SECOM, steel, power, gas, RT-IoT), four clients were simulated by Dirichlet label skew with concentration 0.5 and are described as simulated partitions. Bosch was evaluated in three designs: three machine clients with random roles; a later-period design in which recordings to February 2020 form the federation and later recordings are tested; and leave-one-machine-out, in which two machines form the federation and the third is unseen. HAI used sessions test1 to test3 as clients with roles assigned in contiguous ten-minute blocks, and sessions test4 and test5 recorded two weeks later as a later-session test. The gas-turbine federation used 2011 to 2013, with 2014 and 2015 as out-of-period tests. Feature scaling used medians and interquartile ranges from pooled training rows, which we treat as public metadata; this is disclosed as an assumption.")
    B.append({"type": "table_ref", "id": "datasets"})
    h2("Models and federated training")
    p("All neural models were three-layer perceptrons with 64 and 32 hidden units, trained for 25 rounds of two local epochs with size-weighted averaging and batch size 64 (256 for RT-IoT because of its size). Baselines were a constant reference predictor, class-weighted logistic or ridge regression, a pooled centralised network, local-only networks per client, FedAvg {mcmahan2017}, FedProx {li2020}, SCAFFOLD {karimireddy2020}, FedAvg with coordinate-wise median and with 25 per cent trimmed-mean aggregation {yin2018}, and DP-FedAvg at target budgets ε ∈ {1, 2, 4, 8} with δ = 10^-5^. Class weights were fixed from public prevalence figures so that the training loss did not depend on private label counts. Non-private local updates used batch-level gradient-norm clipping at 5 for numerical stability. Learning rates (and the FedProx coefficient, DP batch size, momentum and class weight) were selected on a separate tuning seed using an internal split of training rows only; the choices are listed in Appendix D and «T:settings». The same records appear under different random partitions in tuning and confirmatory runs, and the privacy cost of tuning {papernot2022} is not included in the reported budgets.")
    p("Opacus could not be installed in the execution environment, so DP-SGD was implemented in NumPy. For a linear layer the per-example gradient is an outer product whose Frobenius norm is the product of the activation and error norms, so exact per-example clipping of the full parameter vector is computed without materialising per-example tensors. Each step used Poisson sampling at rate q = B/n~k~, clipping norm C = 1 and Gaussian noise with standard deviation σC. The noise multiplier σ for each client was set by bisection so that Google's dp_accounting RDP accountant for the Poisson-subsampled Gaussian mechanism reported the target ε after all local steps of all rounds; budgets compose over every step rather than restarting each round. Unit tests check the gradient against finite differences, the per-example norms against explicit computation, the noise scale and the calibrated budgets (Appendix F).")
    B.append({"type": "table_ref", "id": "settings"})
    h2("Secure aggregation")
    p("We implemented the semi-honest pairwise-mask protocol of Bonawitz and colleagues without dropout recovery {bonawitz2017}. Each pair of clients derives a key by X25519 agreement and HKDF-SHA256, expands it with ChaCha20 into 32-bit masks, and each client adds or subtracts its pair masks so that they cancel in the modular sum. Model parameters are encoded in fixed point with scale 2^20^ after weighting by the public client share. The server learns only the sum; a client dropping out after masking leaves the sum unrecoverable, which a test confirms. The protocol runs in one process and therefore measures computation and message volume, not network latency. Secure aggregation protects against an honest-but-curious server; it does not protect against a malicious server, collusion with clients or poisoned inputs, and it prevents the server from inspecting individual updates.")
    h2("Private aggregate risk control")
    p("Let s(x) ∈ [0, 1] be the fault score of the shared model and b~0~ < b~1~ < … < b~B~ a grid fixed before seeing data (26 bins equally spaced on the logit scale between −8 and 8, with two open end bins). Each client k counts its calibration positives and negatives per bin and adds independent Skellam(μ/K, μ/K) noise to every count. Because the sum of independent Skellam variables is Skellam, the secure sum over K clients carries Skellam(μ, μ) noise per bin. The server forms the noisy cumulative count of positives below the j-th edge, m̃~j~, and the noisy total of positives Ñ~1~. The miss risk of an automatic-pass threshold t is R(t) = P(s(X) < t | Y = 1), which rises with t. For each j in increasing order the server computes")
    eq("p~j~ = P( Bin(n⁻, α~miss~) + Z~j~ ≤ m̃~j~ ),   Z~j~ ~ Skellam(jμ, jμ),   n⁻ = Ñ~1~ − q~1−δc~(Skellam(Bμ, Bμ))")
    p("by exact convolution and admits bin j while p~j~ ≤ δ~test~. Without noise p~j~ reduces to the exact binomial tail that underlies the Clopper–Pearson bound {clopper1934}. The automatic-pass threshold t~low~ is the upper edge of the last admitted bin. The automatic-alarm threshold t~high~ is certified in the same way from the top of the negative histogram with target α~fa~. Scores below t~low~ pass, scores at or above t~high~ raise an alarm, and the remainder are escalated. «F:architecture» shows the complete protocol. The same scan certifies selective classification for steel (risk: an accepted prediction that is wrong) and prediction intervals for regression (risk: an absolute residual outside the interval). We used α~miss~ = 0.10, α~fa~ = 0.05, α = 0.10 for selection and intervals, δ~test~ = 0.04 and δ~count~ = 0.01 per side, fixed before the confirmatory runs.")
    p("**Proposition 1 (validity).** Suppose calibration positives and future positives are exchangeable, the grid is fixed in advance and the noise is as stated. Then P(R(t~low~) > α~miss~) ≤ δ~test~ + δ~count~, and the same bound holds for the false-alarm rate, so both targets hold jointly with probability at least 1 − 2(δ~test~ + δ~count~) = 0.90. The proof (Appendix A) uses three facts: the first bin index j∗ at which the true risk exceeds α~miss~ is fixed, so an error requires rejecting that single null; the binomial-Skellam convolution is a super-uniform p-value because Bin(n, R) is stochastically larger than Bin(n, α) when R > α; and replacing the true count n~1~ by its lower confidence bound n⁻ can only increase the p-value.")
    p("**Proposition 2 (privacy of the pipeline).** Under add-or-remove adjacency, if DP-FedAvg gives each training record an (ε~T~, δ) guarantee and the Skellam histogram release gives each calibration record an (ε~C~, δ) guarantee, then the released model and noisy histograms together satisfy (max(ε~T~, ε~C~), δ) for every record, provided roles are assigned by independent per-record coin flips. A record influences either the training mechanism or the calibration mechanism directly, never both, and the calibration release depends on the training data only through the already released model. We calibrate μ so that the exact hockey-stick divergence of the shifted Skellam pair gives ε~C~ = ε~T~. If records could be used in both roles, sequential RDP composition applies and we report that value as well.")
    p("**Proposition 3 (evidence floor).** Without noise, no automatic-pass region can be certified unless the calibration set holds at least ⌈ln δ~test~ / ln(1 − α~miss~)⌉ = 31 faults at the chosen settings, because even zero missed faults among fewer cases leave a p-value above δ~test~. With noise we report the smallest count at which a noisy count of zero is admitted at the lower count bound, computed exactly. The floor depends only on the evidence and the targets, so it binds every certified method including non-private ones.")
    p("Ablations remove one component at a time: the private plug-in rule admits bins while m̃~j~/Ñ~1~ ≤ α; the uncorrected rule applies the exact binomial test to noisy counts as if they were exact; and the non-private rules (plug-in and Learn-then-Test) use exact counts. A default rule passes scores below 0.5 and alarms above. Private histograms also yield binned probabilities (p̂ = (pos + 0.5)/(pos + neg + 1) per bin) at no extra privacy cost, which we compare with raw scores by expected calibration error (ECE).")
    B.append({"type": "figure_ref", "id": "architecture"})
    h2("Threat model and attacks")
    p("The privacy adversary is an honest-but-curious server or an outside party with access to the released model, which may run membership inference. We evaluated the loss-threshold attack {yeom2017} on members (training rows) and non-members (test rows), reporting area under the curve, the maximum true-minus-false positive difference (attack advantage) and the true positive rate at 1 per cent false positives {carlini2021}, overall and for the rare class. For update leakage we recovered single records analytically from first-layer gradients of a one-record update, without protection, with clipping only and with clipping and Gaussian noise {zhu2019,geiping2020}. The integrity adversary controls one client and sends a sign-flipped update scaled by four, or controls one client's calibration counts and adds fabricated high-score faults. We also flipped 30 per cent of one client's labels and halved participation, conditions that earlier work by the author examined for federated credit-risk models {enang2026}, corrupted test features with Gaussian noise or missing values, varied heterogeneity, client count and participation, and varied the bin count. These attacks are bounded laboratory simulations; they do not represent an adaptive adversary.")
    h2("Outcomes and statistical analysis")
    p("Primary utility metrics were precision-recall area under the curve (PR-AUC) for binary tasks, macro F1 for steel and root mean square error (RMSE) in physical units for regression, with balanced accuracy, Matthews correlation, rare-event recall, Brier score, log loss, ECE, mean absolute error and R^2^ as secondary metrics. Decision metrics were the realised miss rate among test faults, the false-alarm rate among normal records, the escalation rate, selective joint error and interval coverage. Confirmatory runs used seeds 1 to 10 (1 to 5 for RT-IoT), paired across methods. Seeds vary partitions, initialisation and noise for a fixed dataset; intervals over seeds therefore describe that variation and not variation over industrial sites. We report means with percentile bootstrap intervals (10,000 resamples), paired differences with Wilcoxon signed-rank tests and Benjamini–Hochberg control within each table {benjamini1995}, an exact one-sided McNemar test for H2, a bootstrap test against the five-point margin for H3 and a one-sided Wilcoxon test for H4, with Holm adjustment across H2 to H4 {holm1979}. After the confirmatory runs we added run-clustered versions of the H2 and H4 tests as secondary checks, because model combinations within a run share one data partition; this and the calibration-only sweep are recorded as protocol deviations. H1 was assessed in a Monte Carlo study with 1,000 replications per condition, two known score distributions, 30, 100 and 400 calibration positives and ten negatives per positive.")
    h2("Reproducibility")
    p(f"Code, configuration, tuned hyperparameters, tests, derived data tables and every run output are in the public repository Imomazin/research-projects, folder Industry and Intelligent Systems/Industries/Privacy Trust and Intelligent Systems in Industry Six. The protocol was frozen in commit 1dc2dbf before any confirmatory seed ran; one later deviation is recorded in Protocol Deviations.md. Software versions were Python {N['python']}, NumPy {N['numpy']}, pandas {N['pandas']}, SciPy {N['scipy']}, scikit-learn {N['sklearn']}, dp-accounting 0.6.0 and cryptography {N['cryptography']} on a two-core Linux virtual machine without a GPU.")
    return B
